.TH MKFIFO 1 "2026" "spicule" "spicule User Commands"
.SH NAME
mkfifo \- make FIFO special files
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mkfifo
.RB [ \-m
.IR mode ]
.I file
.RI [ file ]\ .\|.\|.
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B mkfifo
creates a FIFO special file (a named pipe) for each
.I file
operand named on the command line. Multiple operands are processed in
turn; an error on one operand does not stop the rest from being
attempted.
.PP
On a native Linux build, this creates a real FIFO. On a Windows build,
creating a FIFO always fails: the underlying platform provides no
filesystem object that FIFO semantics can be mapped onto, so every
.I file
operand fails with a "not implemented" diagnostic. This applies even
when
.B \-m
is given; the mode argument is still fully parsed and validated before
any operand is attempted, so a malformed
.B \-m
argument is still reported as a usage error rather than being masked
by the platform failure.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-m " mode"
Set the file permission bits of each newly-created FIFO to
.IR mode ,
using the same
.I mode
grammar as
.BR chmod (1).
In symbolic-mode strings, the
.B +
and
.B \-
operators are interpreted relative to an assumed initial mode of
.B a=rw
(not
.BR a=rwx :
a FIFO is not given execute permission by default).
.SH OPERANDS
.TP
.I file
A pathname of a FIFO special file to be created.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
All the specified FIFO special files were created successfully.
.TP
.B >0
An error occurred: an invalid option, a missing operand, a missing
.B \-m
argument, an invalid
.I mode
operand, or a failure creating one or more of the named FIFOs (which,
on a Windows build, is every named FIFO). Processing continues with
the next operand after a creation failure.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR chmod (1),
.BR mkdir (1)
